    {
      "en": "This is water",
      "sw": "Hii ni maji",
      "explanation": "Use ni when you are identifying what something is. Hii ni maji means you are saying \"this thing equals water\" or \"this is water\"."
    },
    {
      "en": "This has water",
      "sw": "Hii ina maji",
      "explanation": "Use ina when the subject has something. Hii ina maji means \"this has water\". The i- prefix agrees with hii here, and -na- carries the possession idea."
    },
    {
      "en": "This is a bottle",
      "sw": "Hii ni chupa",
      "explanation": "Ni classifies or identifies the thing. The sentence says that this object is a bottle, not that it contains or owns a bottle."
    },
    {
      "en": "The bottle has water",
      "sw": "Chupa ina maji",
      "explanation": "Chupa is the subject, and ina shows possession or containment: the bottle has water in it. This is different from Chupa ni maji, which would mean \"the bottle is water\"."
    },
    {
      "en": "This is good",
      "sw": "Hii ni nzuri",
      "explanation": "Ni can also connect a subject with a description. Hii ni nzuri means \"this is good\". The adjective nzuri describes what this thing is like."
    },
    {
      "en": "This means what?",
      "sw": "Hii inamaanisha nini?",
      "explanation": "Inamaanisha contains -na-, but here it is not possession. It is the present-tense part of the verb maanisha, \"to mean\". So the sentence asks \"What does this mean?\""
    },
    {
      "en": "It means",
      "sw": "Ina maana",
      "explanation": "This phrase is built from ina plus maana, \"meaning\". Literally it is close to \"it has meaning\", but naturally it means \"it means\"."
    },
    {
      "en": "The child has water",
      "sw": "Mtoto ana maji",
      "explanation": "Ana is used with a human singular subject: a-na. Mtoto ana maji means \"the child has water\"."
    },
    {
      "en": "The child is good",
      "sw": "Mtoto ni mzuri",
      "explanation": "This uses ni because the sentence describes what the child is like. Mzuri agrees with mtoto as a person-class adjective."
    },
    {
      "en": "This has meaning",
      "sw": "Hii ina maana",
      "explanation": "Here ina maana can be understood more literally as \"this has meaning\". The same words often function naturally as \"this means\" depending on context."
    },
